WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.314
This is the executive
briefing for Book 00 of the

2
00:00:03.314 --> 00:00:07.313
Federal Modernization Workbook,
Orientation and Program.

3
00:00:07.313 --> 00:00:12.097
It runs about thirty minutes, and it's
for the people who sponsor, fund and

4
00:00:12.097 --> 00:00:17.612
answer for modernization: executive
sponsors, CIOs, CISOs and program executives.

5
00:00:17.612 --> 00:00:18.825
One point first.

6
00:00:18.825 --> 00:00:23.062
The workbook is independent technical
guidance, not federal policy.

7
00:00:23.062 --> 00:00:25.826
Where it says a law,
memorandum or directive

8
00:00:25.826 --> 00:00:28.437
requires something, it cites that source.

9
00:00:28.437 --> 00:00:31.986
Everything else, including
the program's phases, gates,

10
00:00:31.986 --> 00:00:36.558
principles and decision rights, is
the workbook's own recommendation:

11
00:00:36.558 --> 00:00:40.500
one way to meet those requirements,
not a requirement itself.

12
00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:41.925
The argument is short.

13
00:00:41.925 --> 00:00:46.462
For twenty years Active Directory
quietly did governance nobody wrote down.

14
00:00:46.462 --> 00:00:51.612
Federal policy and vendor lifecycles
now set clocks the estate can't ignore.

15
00:00:51.612 --> 00:00:56.950
Meeting them one product at a time rebuilds
the same governance gaps in a new place,

16
00:00:56.950 --> 00:01:00.325
so modernization has to run
as one governed program.

17
00:01:00.325 --> 00:01:04.265
I'll take four questions in order:
why now, what the program is,

18
00:01:04.265 --> 00:01:08.125
who decides, and how it runs
from pre-flight to steady state.

19
00:01:08.125 --> 00:01:13.040
Every decision slide names the decision,
its owner, and when it's needed.

20
00:01:13.790 --> 00:01:14.828
Start with why.

21
00:01:14.828 --> 00:01:19.124
Most federal estates still run
the client/server model they built

22
00:01:19.124 --> 00:01:23.265
in the early 2000s, with an Active
Directory forest at the center.

23
00:01:23.265 --> 00:01:27.411
Since Windows 2000, the organizational
unit has grouped objects

24
00:01:27.411 --> 00:01:31.327
for delegating administrative
rights and assigning policy.

25
00:01:31.327 --> 00:01:34.426
So placing a user or a
computer in the tree decided,

26
00:01:34.426 --> 00:01:38.509
at the same moment and with no
separate record, who administers it,

27
00:01:38.509 --> 00:01:42.288
which Group Policy applies to
it, what its groups let it reach,

28
00:01:42.288 --> 00:01:47.202
how it's named in directory-integrated
DNS, and which certificates it receives.

29
00:01:47.202 --> 00:01:50.134
It was efficient, and for
a quarter century agencies

30
00:01:50.134 --> 00:01:53.202
got governance as a side
effect of running a directory.

31
00:01:53.202 --> 00:01:55.302
The cost is that it was never stated.

32
00:01:55.302 --> 00:01:57.440
The cloud estate doesn't inherit it.

33
00:01:57.440 --> 00:02:00.802
Entra ID's administrative
units can't be nested.

34
00:02:00.802 --> 00:02:05.188
Intune applies policy to groups
with no hierarchy, and two policies

35
00:02:05.188 --> 00:02:09.652
that disagree produce a conflict to
resolve, not an inheritance rule.

36
00:02:09.652 --> 00:02:16.353
Access, names and trust move to Conditional
Access, a DDI platform and a target PKI.

37
00:02:16.353 --> 00:02:19.939
Each replacement needs someone to
decide what the old placement meant.

38
00:02:19.939 --> 00:02:23.420
An agency that moves users
and retires the forest without

39
00:02:23.420 --> 00:02:27.190
recovering those decisions
doesn't modernize its governance.

40
00:02:27.190 --> 00:02:28.240
It deletes it.

41
00:02:28.240 --> 00:02:32.177
So the first decision is the
sponsor's, when the charter is signed:

42
00:02:32.177 --> 00:02:37.377
treat modernization as governance
reconstruction, not only a technical migration.

43
00:02:37.377 --> 00:02:40.723
If stakeholders don't accept
that, the charter says,

44
00:02:40.723 --> 00:02:43.940
resistance becomes the
program's main risk.

45
00:02:44.690 --> 00:02:46.652
The second reason is the schedule.

46
00:02:46.652 --> 00:02:51.640
Two kinds of clock now set dates from
outside, and the first is federal policy.

47
00:02:51.640 --> 00:02:55.454
OMB M-22-09, from January 2022,

48
00:02:55.454 --> 00:03:00.277
set Zero Trust objectives
for the end of fiscal 2024.

49
00:03:00.277 --> 00:03:03.925
That date has passed, but
the actions remain in force,

50
00:03:03.925 --> 00:03:06.639
on-premises and hybrid as well as cloud.

51
00:03:06.639 --> 00:03:12.344
CISA's BOD 25-01 set mandatory
configurations for cloud tenants,

52
00:03:12.344 --> 00:03:14.800
with the policies due June 20, 2025,

53
00:03:14.800 --> 00:03:19.802
and every new tenant baselined
before its authorization to operate.

54
00:03:19.802 --> 00:03:26.706
BOD 26-02, from February 2026, directs
agencies to remove internet-facing

55
00:03:26.706 --> 00:03:32.803
end-of-support edge devices; the
listed ones go by February 5, 2027.

56
00:03:32.803 --> 00:03:36.553
OMB M-26-14, issued May 22, 2026,

57
00:03:36.553 --> 00:03:44.215
rescinded M-21-31 and set a
risk-based approach to logging.

58
00:03:44.215 --> 00:03:48.529
And FedRAMP's Consolidated Rules
for 2026, launched June 25,

59
00:03:48.529 --> 00:03:53.019
take mandatory effect for all
stakeholders on January 1, 2027,

60
00:03:53.019 --> 00:03:58.390
and providers then have a year to follow
them to keep their certification.

61
00:03:58.390 --> 00:04:04.915
FedRAMP accepts no new Rev5 certification
applications after June 11, 2027.

62
00:04:04.915 --> 00:04:09.244
M-26-14 counts its deadlines
from CISA's Logging

63
00:04:09.244 --> 00:04:13.790
Reference Architecture,
published August 20, 2026.

64
00:04:13.790 --> 00:04:21.130
Counted from that date, the Agency Logging
Plan falls on November 18 of this year,

65
00:04:21.130 --> 00:04:27.802
Level 1 on December 18, Level 2 on February
16, 2027, and Level 3 on July 6, 2027.

66
00:04:27.802 --> 00:04:32.390
Those four dates are the program's
arithmetic, not dates OMB published.

67
00:04:32.390 --> 00:04:36.163
So the nearest decision: confirm
them with the CISO's office,

68
00:04:36.163 --> 00:04:40.840
starting with the logging plan, due
November 18 by the program's count.

69
00:04:41.590 --> 00:04:44.202
The second set of clocks
is vendor support.

70
00:04:44.202 --> 00:04:49.449
After end of support there are no new
security updates and no assisted support,

71
00:04:49.449 --> 00:04:53.403
and for an estate built in the
early 2000s, the dates cluster.

72
00:04:53.403 --> 00:05:01.064
Windows 10 and Exchange Server 2016 and 2019
ended in October 2025; SQL Server 2016,

73
00:05:01.064 --> 00:05:04.106
SharePoint Server 2016 and 2019,

74
00:05:04.106 --> 00:05:10.303
and the last Windows Server 2012
security updates ended this year.

75
00:05:10.303 --> 00:05:15.447
Windows Server 2016 ends on January
12, 2027, under three months away,

76
00:05:15.447 --> 00:05:21.602
and it's the operating system of many domain
controllers built in the last decade.

77
00:05:21.602 --> 00:05:29.889
Windows Server 2019 and Microsoft Identity
Manager 2016 end on January 9, 2029.

78
00:05:29.889 --> 00:05:33.399
Identity Manager's date
was extended from 2026,

79
00:05:33.399 --> 00:05:38.978
so an agency that syncs HR data through
it has a date, not an open-ended bridge.

80
00:05:38.978 --> 00:05:43.303
A vendor date isn't a federal mandate
on its own, but the two connect.

81
00:05:43.303 --> 00:05:48.737
BOD 26-02 turns end of support into a
directed action for internet-facing edge

82
00:05:48.737 --> 00:05:52.472
devices, and every system
security plan asserts flaw

83
00:05:52.472 --> 00:05:56.378
remediation that an unsupported
product can't deliver.

84
00:05:56.378 --> 00:05:59.078
So running past end of
support is a decision,

85
00:05:59.078 --> 00:06:03.953
and it belongs to the authorizing official,
who has to sign the risk acceptance.

86
00:06:03.953 --> 00:06:09.750
The first of those falls due with
Windows Server 2016, on January 12.

87
00:06:10.500 --> 00:06:13.125
The clocks are often quoted
for more than they say.

88
00:06:13.125 --> 00:06:14.650
Four corrections recur.

89
00:06:14.650 --> 00:06:20.301
FedRAMP certifies cloud services, and each
agency still authorizes its own systems,

90
00:06:20.301 --> 00:06:23.531
so the 2027 date changes
what providers publish,

91
00:06:23.531 --> 00:06:26.437
not whether the agency's
own systems comply.

92
00:06:26.437 --> 00:06:28.961
Moderate is now a certification class:

93
00:06:28.961 --> 00:06:33.376
Class C includes the current Moderate
baseline, and Class D the High.

94
00:06:33.376 --> 00:06:37.337
No federal document requires
a Zero Trust maturity stage.

95
00:06:37.337 --> 00:06:43.068
And BOD 25-01 covers production cloud
tenants, not on-premises Active Directory,

96
00:06:43.068 --> 00:06:47.087
though the forest still decides
much of what a tenant can enforce.

97
00:06:47.087 --> 00:06:49.988
Nothing makes the estate
fail on a single date.

98
00:06:49.988 --> 00:06:53.974
It's surrounded by deadlines, each
binding a different part of it,

99
00:06:53.974 --> 00:06:58.788
and each assuming the agency knows what
it runs, who owns it and what governs it.

100
00:06:58.788 --> 00:07:01.012
That's why it can't be a set of projects.

101
00:07:01.012 --> 00:07:05.201
Moving devices to Intune needs
the policy GPO links carried;

102
00:07:05.201 --> 00:07:08.887
Conditional Access needs
the access groups granted.

103
00:07:08.887 --> 00:07:13.263
Separate projects rediscover those
decisions differently, or not at all.

104
00:07:13.263 --> 00:07:16.257
A program can sequence the
work against the dates;

105
00:07:16.257 --> 00:07:18.887
a collection of projects
can only race them.

106
00:07:18.887 --> 00:07:23.708
Hence the decision for the sponsor and
CIO, before the budget lines are set:

107
00:07:23.708 --> 00:07:27.422
fund and govern modernization
as one sequenced program,

108
00:07:27.422 --> 00:07:30.790
with one dependency chain
and one set of gates.

109
00:07:31.540 --> 00:07:33.015
Now, what the program is.

110
00:07:33.015 --> 00:07:36.306
It starts with a short charter:
the page the sponsor signs,

111
00:07:36.306 --> 00:07:40.928
and the page every later decision is
checked against: is this inside the scope we

112
00:07:40.928 --> 00:07:43.589
chartered, does it serve
one of the objectives,

113
00:07:43.589 --> 00:07:45.690
and does it move a success criterion?

114
00:07:45.690 --> 00:07:48.728
The problem it sets out
to remove has four costs:

115
00:07:48.728 --> 00:07:52.233
migration risk concentrated
in hidden dependencies;

116
00:07:52.233 --> 00:07:56.517
ongoing drift between intended
and actual state; weak evidence of

117
00:07:56.517 --> 00:08:01.190
change and compliance; and a high cost
of reconciling fragmented stores.

118
00:08:01.190 --> 00:08:03.515
Against those, six objectives.

119
00:08:03.515 --> 00:08:07.092
One authoritative origin for
organizational structure,

120
00:08:07.092 --> 00:08:11.190
identity attributes and key claims,
with every other copy derived.

121
00:08:11.190 --> 00:08:14.613
Governance rebuilt explicitly,
through attributes,

122
00:08:14.613 --> 00:08:17.102
dynamic membership and scoped policy.

123
00:08:17.102 --> 00:08:19.577
Drift treated as a classified finding.

124
00:08:19.577 --> 00:08:23.717
Change in controlled waves that
start from the authoritative source,

125
00:08:23.717 --> 00:08:26.715
run under least privilege,
and close with evidence.

126
00:08:26.715 --> 00:08:29.728
Domain transformations
without rip-and-replace.

127
00:08:29.728 --> 00:08:32.490
And continuous, machine-usable evidence.

128
00:08:32.490 --> 00:08:34.140
The scope is deliberate.

129
00:08:34.140 --> 00:08:37.903
The program feeds compliance
frameworks; it doesn't replace them.

130
00:08:37.903 --> 00:08:41.536
Identity providers, network
fabrics and device managers

131
00:08:41.536 --> 00:08:44.564
stay the enforcers, and
the program governs them.

132
00:08:44.564 --> 00:08:47.753
And the adopting agency
executes its own cutover.

133
00:08:47.753 --> 00:08:50.453
An agency can adopt
the charter as written.

134
00:08:50.453 --> 00:08:55.146
The decision: the sponsor signs it,
and names go into its governance table,

135
00:08:55.146 --> 00:08:59.650
starting with the sponsor and the
steward, as Phase 0's entry work.

136
00:09:00.400 --> 00:09:03.236
A sponsor can't sign off
an outcome with no test,

137
00:09:03.236 --> 00:09:06.893
so the charter turns each
success criterion into a measure,

138
00:09:06.893 --> 00:09:10.400
the evidence that shows it, and
the gate where it's checked.

139
00:09:10.400 --> 00:09:16.091
First, a current-state assessment that
covers every forest in scope and all eleven

140
00:09:16.091 --> 00:09:21.363
dependency object types, with zero open
priority-one findings, checked at G0.

141
00:09:21.363 --> 00:09:25.314
Second, a target structure in
which every attribute that

142
00:09:25.314 --> 00:09:29.687
drives access has exactly one
designated source, checked at G1.

143
00:09:29.687 --> 00:09:34.850
Third, a domain playbook whose entry and
exit criteria are written as queries,

144
00:09:34.850 --> 00:09:39.771
proven when the pilot division clears
G2 with zero drift for a hold period;

145
00:09:39.771 --> 00:09:42.675
fourteen days is the illustrative example.

146
00:09:42.675 --> 00:09:48.056
Fourth, a change-control pattern in use:
every pilot change carries all five clauses

147
00:09:48.056 --> 00:09:53.125
of the change lifecycle, and every human-only
closure is logged as an exception.

148
00:09:53.125 --> 00:09:56.231
Fifth, drift classes
with a detection signal,

149
00:09:56.231 --> 00:10:01.100
a severity and a response, where a
missed run is itself a halt condition.

150
00:10:01.100 --> 00:10:03.493
Sixth, leadership can describe the path,

151
00:10:03.493 --> 00:10:07.700
the risks and the measures without
citing internal decision records.

152
00:10:07.700 --> 00:10:11.860
The numbers are illustrative
defaults: the agency sets its own,

153
00:10:11.860 --> 00:10:14.900
records them, and doesn't
change them silently.

154
00:10:14.900 --> 00:10:17.650
And the criteria stop at
the pilot on purpose.

155
00:10:17.650 --> 00:10:20.262
The sixth criterion is the sponsor's own.

156
00:10:20.262 --> 00:10:23.724
The sponsor answers yes to
each pre-start question by G0,

157
00:10:23.724 --> 00:10:27.840
and the steering committee
reviews the drift trend monthly.

158
00:10:28.590 --> 00:10:32.803
The charter's six guiding principles
expand to fifteen in Chapter 04,

159
00:10:32.803 --> 00:10:35.095
and one idea runs underneath them all:

160
00:10:35.095 --> 00:10:39.752
keep the record of what should be true
apart from the systems that enforce it,

161
00:10:39.752 --> 00:10:43.152
and make every change prove
itself against that record.

162
00:10:43.152 --> 00:10:46.403
The foundation principles
apply to every change.

163
00:10:46.403 --> 00:10:49.053
Every claim has one authoritative origin.

164
00:10:49.053 --> 00:10:53.386
Every move, add, change, deletion
and reset travels five clauses:

165
00:10:53.386 --> 00:10:58.971
it originates with the source of truth,
carries an authoritative authorization,

166
00:10:58.971 --> 00:11:02.055
executes under just-in-time
least privilege,

167
00:11:02.055 --> 00:11:06.139
closes with provenance, and
emits evidence automatically.

168
00:11:06.139 --> 00:11:08.952
No closure counts unless
it carries its proof.

169
00:11:08.952 --> 00:11:11.428
And drift detection
re-checks on a schedule.

170
00:11:11.428 --> 00:11:16.692
The structure and estate principles replace
the organizational unit (OU) tree with

171
00:11:16.692 --> 00:11:21.652
attributes, and they carry one rule
executives should hear: a user with an empty

172
00:11:21.652 --> 00:11:24.246
attribute falls out of the dynamic group,

173
00:11:24.246 --> 00:11:26.840
and so out of every
policy that targets it.

174
00:11:26.840 --> 00:11:29.790
Attribute completeness
is a security control.

175
00:11:29.790 --> 00:11:32.035
The program principles cover people,

176
00:11:32.035 --> 00:11:35.077
Zero Trust governance and
the direction of travel.

177
00:11:35.077 --> 00:11:39.941
Two source rules were corrected on the
way: Intune policy conflicts, and token

178
00:11:39.941 --> 00:11:42.829
lifetimes, where the
principle that survives

179
00:11:42.829 --> 00:11:45.565
is continuous evaluation,
not a short clock.

180
00:11:45.565 --> 00:11:50.255
The decision here is the steward's, and
it comes before the first change is made:

181
00:11:50.255 --> 00:11:55.530
set the evidence and provenance expectations
that every later change must meet.

182
00:11:56.280 --> 00:11:59.612
A plan earns its keep by saying
what has to be true before

183
00:11:59.612 --> 00:12:02.655
the next step starts, not by
putting dates on a chart.

184
00:12:02.655 --> 00:12:06.335
The Federal Modernization
Workbook's Book 01 of the

185
00:12:06.335 --> 00:12:09.843
workbook is canonical:
seven phases and six gates.

186
00:12:09.843 --> 00:12:14.106
Phase 0, Pre-flight, ends
at G0, Ready to Plan.

187
00:12:14.106 --> 00:12:18.667
Phase 1, Foundation, ends
at G1, Foundation Complete.

188
00:12:18.667 --> 00:12:23.068
Phase 2, the pilot division,
ends at G2, Pilot Clean.

189
00:12:23.068 --> 00:12:27.667
Phase 3, general rollout,
at G3, Rollout Complete.

190
00:12:27.667 --> 00:12:32.080
Phase 4, legacy retirement,
at G4, Legacy Quiet.

191
00:12:32.080 --> 00:12:37.117
Phase 5, AD DS retirement,
at G5, AD Retired.

192
00:12:37.117 --> 00:12:41.268
Phase 6, steady state, runs
on a cadence indefinitely.

193
00:12:41.268 --> 00:12:43.956
The gates are owned in
proportion to what they end.

194
00:12:43.956 --> 00:12:50.780
The steward clears G0, G1 and G4, and
co-signs G2 with the executive sponsor.

195
00:12:50.780 --> 00:12:53.943
The sponsor and steering
committee clear G3.

196
00:12:53.943 --> 00:12:57.230
The sponsor and the CIO clear G5.

197
00:12:57.230 --> 00:12:59.904
And five constraints bind every phase,

198
00:12:59.904 --> 00:13:03.580
from provenance on every
change to no rip-and-replace.

199
00:13:03.580 --> 00:13:08.455
The program's detailed master plan
sequences 48 milestones over an

200
00:13:08.455 --> 00:13:13.330
illustrative 52 weeks, but its
cutover ends with AD DS still running.

201
00:13:13.330 --> 00:13:17.118
Book 01's own fifty-two-week
sequence models a mid-size,

202
00:13:17.118 --> 00:13:21.768
clean forest, and large federal
estates typically run multi-year.

203
00:13:21.768 --> 00:13:26.338
So the decision for the sponsor
and CIO, when they accept the plan:

204
00:13:26.338 --> 00:13:31.520
budget the AD DS retirement phase on
top of the master plan's 52 weeks.

205
00:13:32.270 --> 00:13:36.175
Inside the phases, five
workstreams carry the work:

206
00:13:36.175 --> 00:13:39.708
identity, policy, services,
compute and access.

207
00:13:39.708 --> 00:13:42.044
They run together; finishing one before

208
00:13:42.044 --> 00:13:44.807
starting the next roughly
doubles the program.

209
00:13:44.807 --> 00:13:46.120
They still have an order.

210
00:13:46.120 --> 00:13:49.052
Identity comes first, the attribute model,

211
00:13:49.052 --> 00:13:53.826
dynamic membership and the lifecycle
origin, because policy, access,

212
00:13:53.826 --> 00:13:58.182
devices and data all decide on the
basis of who or what is asking.

213
00:13:58.182 --> 00:13:59.894
Policy follows identity.

214
00:13:59.894 --> 00:14:04.745
Services, naming and addressing,
can run in parallel and may bridge.

215
00:14:04.745 --> 00:14:08.395
Compute, the device and server
work, comes mid to late.

216
00:14:08.395 --> 00:14:12.157
Access, token-based and
application-aware, comes later,

217
00:14:12.157 --> 00:14:15.333
because it consumes both
identity and policy.

218
00:14:15.333 --> 00:14:19.506
The master plan organized the same
work as five technology pillars,

219
00:14:19.506 --> 00:14:21.295
and that view leaves two gaps.

220
00:14:21.295 --> 00:14:25.339
Nobody owns the question of which
policy surface owns a setting,

221
00:14:25.339 --> 00:14:27.857
and the access plane has no pillar at all.

222
00:14:27.857 --> 00:14:32.870
The Federal Modernization Workbook's
Book 01 closes them by giving policy

223
00:14:32.870 --> 00:14:37.883
a security-engineering lead and access
a joint identity and security lead.

224
00:14:37.883 --> 00:14:41.680
The critical path runs through
the platform, the assessment,

225
00:14:41.680 --> 00:14:46.694
attribute and group design, the pilot,
each rollout wave, and cutover validation.

226
00:14:46.694 --> 00:14:51.583
Every week lost in assessment or
attribute design moves every later gate.

227
00:14:51.583 --> 00:14:55.882
And one dependency never
relaxes: PKI waits for identity.

228
00:14:55.882 --> 00:15:01.028
The decision, made while Phase 0's stakeholders
and decision rights are confirmed:

229
00:15:01.028 --> 00:15:04.630
name the five workstream
leads, including those two.

230
00:15:05.380 --> 00:15:10.011
Seven complementary projects turn
the program's most common gaps

231
00:15:10.011 --> 00:15:14.305
into executable work, each with
its own sponsor, lead and pilot.

232
00:15:14.305 --> 00:15:18.766
In their suggested starting order:
Identity Lifecycle and Provisioning;

233
00:15:18.766 --> 00:15:23.082
Endpoint and Device Modernization;
Network and Access Modernization;

234
00:15:23.082 --> 00:15:26.007
Continuous Monitoring
and the Evidence Fabric;

235
00:15:26.007 --> 00:15:30.469
Privileged Access and Administrative
Identity; Data Platform Identity

236
00:15:30.469 --> 00:15:34.418
Transformation; and Supply Chain
and Software Bill of Materials.

237
00:15:34.418 --> 00:15:36.831
The order says which project has to be far

238
00:15:36.831 --> 00:15:39.529
enough along before the
next can show results.

239
00:15:39.529 --> 00:15:44.749
Identity is first, because everything
else consumes its attributes and owners;

240
00:15:44.749 --> 00:15:49.730
a device project started on unreliable
attributes produces policy bypass.

241
00:15:49.730 --> 00:15:52.974
Monitoring comes fourth,
because an evidence fabric

242
00:15:52.974 --> 00:15:56.217
needs at least two sources
already emitting records.

243
00:15:56.217 --> 00:16:00.280
Privileged access is chartered fifth,
but it isn't fifth in practice:

244
00:16:00.280 --> 00:16:05.211
the Foundation gate already needs emergency
access accounts vaulted and tested,

245
00:16:05.211 --> 00:16:09.418
and Tier 0 administrators registered
for phishing-resistant sign-in.

246
00:16:09.418 --> 00:16:13.268
Every charter asks for a working
pilot, not a finished estate.

247
00:16:13.268 --> 00:16:15.718
Two rules keep the portfolio coherent.

248
00:16:15.718 --> 00:16:18.487
No project designates
a second authoritative

249
00:16:18.487 --> 00:16:21.043
source for something another project owns.

250
00:16:21.043 --> 00:16:26.056
And every project emits evidence in the
shape the monitoring project defines.

251
00:16:26.056 --> 00:16:29.281
The projects don't form a
second governance structure,

252
00:16:29.281 --> 00:16:32.005
and no project clears a
program gate on its own.

253
00:16:32.005 --> 00:16:35.151
Filling in their names is
each project's first act,

254
00:16:35.151 --> 00:16:38.440
and every project lead
reports to the steward.

255
00:16:39.190 --> 00:16:40.215
Who decides?

256
00:16:40.215 --> 00:16:43.354
The book's advice: name the
people allowed to say no,

257
00:16:43.354 --> 00:16:48.178
give each decision one of them, and make
the repository remember who said yes.

258
00:16:48.178 --> 00:16:53.452
The book uses the Federal Modernization
Workbook's Book 01's program-wide model.

259
00:16:53.452 --> 00:16:58.207
The executive sponsor, with the CIO,
owns scope, funding and sequence,

260
00:16:58.207 --> 00:17:02.877
breaks ties, and decides when
Active Directory components retire.

261
00:17:02.877 --> 00:17:07.397
The CISO sets the control requirements
every target surface must meet:

262
00:17:07.397 --> 00:17:11.690
authentication strength, the
Conditional Access baseline, logging.

263
00:17:11.690 --> 00:17:14.621
The authorizing official
is the only official

264
00:17:14.621 --> 00:17:17.177
who can accept security and privacy risk.

265
00:17:17.177 --> 00:17:20.737
The steward leads the program
office and owns the plan,

266
00:17:20.737 --> 00:17:24.928
wave sequencing, cross-domain
dependencies and the repository.

267
00:17:24.928 --> 00:17:29.560
A change to the target architecture, or
to the rules for authoritative origin,

268
00:17:29.560 --> 00:17:32.165
needs the steward and
the sponsor together.

269
00:17:32.165 --> 00:17:35.703
HR decides what each
organizational attribute means.

270
00:17:35.703 --> 00:17:40.514
And workstream leads own their designs,
inside the CISO's requirements.

271
00:17:40.514 --> 00:17:44.790
Two federal requirements already name
people who belong in these rows.

272
00:17:44.790 --> 00:17:50.225
M-22-09 required a zero trust
implementation lead within 30 days,

273
00:17:50.225 --> 00:17:51.753
and an aligned C-suite.

274
00:17:51.753 --> 00:17:57.677
M-19-17 requires an integrated,
agency-wide ICAM governance structure.

275
00:17:57.677 --> 00:18:01.978
So the decision for the sponsor and
CIO, in Phase 0: name the sponsor,

276
00:18:01.978 --> 00:18:04.607
the steward and the steering committee,

277
00:18:04.607 --> 00:18:08.960
and build them from those existing
roles, not alongside them.

278
00:18:09.710 --> 00:18:13.098
Most of what the program's plans
said about decisions holds.

279
00:18:13.098 --> 00:18:14.360
Three items don't.

280
00:18:14.360 --> 00:18:15.672
Residual risk.

281
00:18:15.672 --> 00:18:19.423
The plans let the security lead
and the steward accept it jointly.

282
00:18:19.423 --> 00:18:22.963
Under the Risk Management
Framework, only the authorizing

283
00:18:22.963 --> 00:18:27.010
official can accept security and
privacy risk to the organization.

284
00:18:27.010 --> 00:18:30.605
So the security lead and
steward prepare and recommend,

285
00:18:30.605 --> 00:18:35.998
and the acceptance carries a justification,
compensating controls and an end date.

286
00:18:35.998 --> 00:18:37.459
Budget and licenses.

287
00:18:37.459 --> 00:18:41.060
They belong to the sponsor
and CIO, who own funding.

288
00:18:41.060 --> 00:18:42.985
The steward prepares the case.

289
00:18:42.985 --> 00:18:44.235
Gate decisions.

290
00:18:44.235 --> 00:18:48.510
The master plan made the steward
the sole signatory at every gate.

291
00:18:48.510 --> 00:18:53.854
The Federal Modernization Workbook's Book
01 spreads the gates across the steward,

292
00:18:53.854 --> 00:18:56.875
the sponsor, the steering
committee and the CIO,

293
00:18:56.875 --> 00:18:59.198
in proportion to what each gate ends.

294
00:18:59.198 --> 00:19:01.711
Four separations keep
the governance honest.

295
00:19:01.711 --> 00:19:06.775
The authorizing official is a government
official, the decision can't be delegated,

296
00:19:06.775 --> 00:19:09.565
and the role stays outside
the program office:

297
00:19:09.565 --> 00:19:13.235
contractors can prepare a risk
acceptance, but can't sign one.

298
00:19:13.235 --> 00:19:15.635
No one holds two roles
that check each other.

299
00:19:15.635 --> 00:19:18.210
Requesters don't approve
their own changes.

300
00:19:18.210 --> 00:19:21.660
And the component that decides
isn't the one that acts.

301
00:19:21.660 --> 00:19:25.581
The decision sits with the
authorizing official from G0 onward,

302
00:19:25.581 --> 00:19:30.095
where an unremediated Critical finding
needs the official's acceptance:

303
00:19:30.095 --> 00:19:33.860
every risk acceptance comes
in writing, with an end date.

304
00:19:34.610 --> 00:19:35.635
Who does the work?

305
00:19:35.635 --> 00:19:41.165
The master plan estimated the program
office and workstreams at about five and a

306
00:19:41.165 --> 00:19:46.530
half full-time equivalents, for an
illustrative 52-week program: half a steward;

307
00:19:46.530 --> 00:19:50.987
a full-time infrastructure lead,
identity lead and endpoint lead;

308
00:19:50.987 --> 00:19:55.197
and half-time DNS, PKI and security
leads and a project manager.

309
00:19:55.197 --> 00:19:57.909
It's a planning model,
not a staffing standard.

310
00:19:57.909 --> 00:20:01.023
What matters more is what
the estimate leaves out:

311
00:20:01.023 --> 00:20:04.440
application owners for every
application that moves,

312
00:20:04.440 --> 00:20:08.616
help desk capacity for every wave,
HR and contractor-source owners,

313
00:20:08.616 --> 00:20:11.881
records and privacy, and
the CISO's and authorizing

314
00:20:11.881 --> 00:20:15.222
official's time for requirements
and risk decisions.

315
00:20:15.222 --> 00:20:19.285
The program can't run without them,
so budget for them explicitly.

316
00:20:19.285 --> 00:20:21.610
And cross-train two people per workstream.

317
00:20:21.610 --> 00:20:23.516
The rhythm: daily standups,

318
00:20:23.516 --> 00:20:28.022
and a weekly report rating each
workstream green, yellow or red.

319
00:20:28.022 --> 00:20:30.910
The steering committee
meets every two weeks,

320
00:20:30.910 --> 00:20:34.407
the steward and sponsor review
gate readiness monthly,

321
00:20:34.407 --> 00:20:39.347
and each gate gets a ninety-minute review
that ends in go, no-go or conditional go.

322
00:20:39.347 --> 00:20:43.334
And two consecutive red weeks for
any workstream go to the sponsor.

323
00:20:43.334 --> 00:20:48.664
The decision for the sponsor and CIO: put
those people in the Phase 0 business case,

324
00:20:48.664 --> 00:20:53.560
alongside the licenses, and approve
the budget before design begins.

325
00:20:54.310 --> 00:20:57.210
Now, how it runs,
starting with pre-flight.

326
00:20:57.210 --> 00:21:01.898
The program adopts one rule
unchanged: assessment before action.

327
00:21:01.898 --> 00:21:06.560
No migration work starts until the
whole forest is inventoried, classified

328
00:21:06.560 --> 00:21:11.148
and committed to the repository, and
every plan is generated from that data.

329
00:21:11.148 --> 00:21:15.876
First, the program builds the place where
every decision will be written down:

330
00:21:15.876 --> 00:21:19.422
a platform host with one
authoritative, protected writer.

331
00:21:19.422 --> 00:21:23.187
The reference design is
Gitea on Windows Server 2025,

332
00:21:23.187 --> 00:21:26.523
one way to meet the
requirement, not the only one.

333
00:21:26.523 --> 00:21:30.166
The host is a Tier 0 system,
and a branch protection the

334
00:21:30.166 --> 00:21:34.223
steward can quietly override is
a convention, not a control.

335
00:21:34.223 --> 00:21:38.073
Then it reads the estate:
read-only first, at default rights,

336
00:21:38.073 --> 00:21:42.084
then narrow delegated additions
and a security assessment.

337
00:21:42.084 --> 00:21:46.825
The master plan's twelve Active
Directory domains are joined by the DHCP,

338
00:21:46.825 --> 00:21:52.277
LDAP-binding and service-principal-name
streams that the Federal Modernization

339
00:21:52.277 --> 00:21:56.148
Workbook's Book 01 adds, and by
an inventory of RADIUS and NPS,

340
00:21:56.148 --> 00:21:58.360
which depend on the directory too.

341
00:21:58.360 --> 00:22:03.112
The phase delivers twenty-three
artifacts: fifteen committed before G0,

342
00:22:03.112 --> 00:22:06.360
and eight generated plans
approved just after it.

343
00:22:06.360 --> 00:22:10.340
G0 has three conditions: the
platform host is operational,

344
00:22:10.340 --> 00:22:14.735
the assessment is committed, and
no priority-one finding is open.

345
00:22:14.735 --> 00:22:17.655
Every finding is classed Critical, High,

346
00:22:17.655 --> 00:22:21.460
Medium or Low, and each class
has a gate that closes it.

347
00:22:21.460 --> 00:22:24.448
A Critical finding is
remediated before G0,

348
00:22:24.448 --> 00:22:27.935
or formally accepted by
the authorizing official.

349
00:22:27.935 --> 00:22:33.390
The steward clears G0 on that basis,
with the executive sponsor as backup.

350
00:22:34.140 --> 00:22:37.133
Phase 1, Foundation, opens
by generating the eight

351
00:22:37.133 --> 00:22:40.565
plans from the committed
assessment and approving them.

352
00:22:40.565 --> 00:22:46.225
It then designs every surface from
those plans, attributes, dynamic groups,

353
00:22:46.225 --> 00:22:50.060
Intune policy, Conditional
Access, sync, DNS and PKI,

354
00:22:50.060 --> 00:22:53.803
and builds the shared
foundation the pilot needs.

355
00:22:53.803 --> 00:22:57.052
Group processing is planned
in hours, not minutes.

356
00:22:57.052 --> 00:23:00.939
The steward clears G1 when the
plans and designs are approved,

357
00:23:00.939 --> 00:23:05.968
the baseline Conditional Access policies
are enforced after at least a week in

358
00:23:05.968 --> 00:23:10.160
report-only, the emergency access
accounts are vaulted and tested,

359
00:23:10.160 --> 00:23:13.665
the HR feed is live, and every
High finding is remediated.

360
00:23:13.665 --> 00:23:16.440
Phase 2 proves the whole
pattern on real people.

361
00:23:16.440 --> 00:23:21.917
The Federal Modernization Workbook's Book
01 sets the pilot's scope as one division,

362
00:23:21.917 --> 00:23:25.013
typically IT, which
tolerates disruption best,

363
00:23:25.013 --> 00:23:29.140
and the master plan's sizes are a
floor for what it must exercise.

364
00:23:29.140 --> 00:23:34.065
It's measured against eight criteria,
from sign-in success to help desk volume.

365
00:23:34.065 --> 00:23:38.985
The values are illustrative: set them in
the decision log before the pilot opens,

366
00:23:38.985 --> 00:23:42.190
and evaluate them from the
systems, not from opinion.

367
00:23:42.190 --> 00:23:45.590
Every surface has a rollback
trigger and a procedure.

368
00:23:45.590 --> 00:23:51.098
G2 is signed jointly by the steward and
the executive sponsor: zero drift for the

369
00:23:51.098 --> 00:23:57.050
agreed hold period, every criterion met,
and no hybrid-joined PC without a cut date.

370
00:23:57.800 --> 00:24:03.675
Phase 3, general rollout, repeats the proven
pattern until every division has moved.

371
00:24:03.675 --> 00:24:07.228
A wave is a set of divisions
chosen by attribute value,

372
00:24:07.228 --> 00:24:10.700
usually by region, with the
lowest-risk region first.

373
00:24:10.700 --> 00:24:13.767
Group Policy links stay
in place during a wave,

374
00:24:13.767 --> 00:24:18.288
and come off a migrated OU only
after seven days of clean compliance.

375
00:24:18.288 --> 00:24:21.165
The sponsor and steering
committee clear G3

376
00:24:21.165 --> 00:24:24.275
when every division has
passed its own exit gate.

377
00:24:24.275 --> 00:24:28.400
Then the work changes character:
these phases take old things away.

378
00:24:28.400 --> 00:24:33.872
Phase 4 retires AD FS, user and device
certificate issuance from AD CS,

379
00:24:33.872 --> 00:24:36.657
DNS forwarders, Group Policy links,

380
00:24:36.657 --> 00:24:41.937
non-essential trusts and legacy
servers, each through its own plan.

381
00:24:41.937 --> 00:24:45.726
A component is retired only
while its rollback still works.

382
00:24:45.726 --> 00:24:50.763
Some things stay until the domain itself
retires: its own DNS zones, the domain

383
00:24:50.763 --> 00:24:55.800
controller and LDAPS certificates, and
the default domain account policies.

384
00:24:55.800 --> 00:24:57.725
The steward clears G4.

385
00:24:57.725 --> 00:25:02.497
Phase 5 retires AD DS itself, which
the master plan never reached,

386
00:25:02.497 --> 00:25:07.088
or reduces it to a documented
Kerberos island with an end date.

387
00:25:07.088 --> 00:25:09.076
Two steps are easy to forget.

388
00:25:09.076 --> 00:25:10.912
Records come before hardware.

389
00:25:10.912 --> 00:25:15.047
And domain controllers store the
password hashes of every account,

390
00:25:15.047 --> 00:25:18.074
so retired hosts and their
storage are sanitized.

391
00:25:18.074 --> 00:25:23.325
The sponsor and CIO clear G5, and they
decide when each component retires,

392
00:25:23.325 --> 00:25:26.560
after the records officer is consulted.

393
00:25:27.310 --> 00:25:30.647
Phase 6, steady state, has no exit gate.

394
00:25:30.647 --> 00:25:35.593
The modernized estate runs under continuous
governance: reassessment weekly,

395
00:25:35.593 --> 00:25:38.815
drift detection daily, a
monthly compliance report,

396
00:25:38.815 --> 00:25:42.636
quarterly access reviews and
an annual architecture review.

397
00:25:42.636 --> 00:25:45.110
The finish line is zero drift findings,

398
00:25:45.110 --> 00:25:50.060
zero actions from the nightly plan run,
and signed evidence flowing to the SIEM.

399
00:25:50.060 --> 00:25:53.897
Every finding gets a severity,
and every severity a clock.

400
00:25:53.897 --> 00:25:57.210
The master plan's defaults
run from a Critical finding

401
00:25:57.210 --> 00:26:00.522
fixed within four hours to a
Low one within thirty days.

402
00:26:00.522 --> 00:26:05.633
The agency sets its own values; what the
program insists on is that there are some,

403
00:26:05.633 --> 00:26:09.736
written into its continuous
monitoring strategy and measured.

404
00:26:09.736 --> 00:26:11.735
Success is measured twice.

405
00:26:11.735 --> 00:26:17.179
Delivery measures turn green gate by
gate: the assessment accepted at G0,

406
00:26:17.179 --> 00:26:23.349
the target model agreed at G1, the first
wave run with full provenance and evidence

407
00:26:23.349 --> 00:26:29.247
at G2, drift detection operating by G2 and
G3, and every residual risk owned by G5.

408
00:26:29.247 --> 00:26:33.559
Outcome measures are what operations
keeps green after the project ends.

409
00:26:33.559 --> 00:26:36.928
A program can meet every
delivery measure and still fail

410
00:26:36.928 --> 00:26:40.510
if the outcome measures drift
after the project team leaves.

411
00:26:40.510 --> 00:26:43.023
That's why the handover
is part of the scorecard.

412
00:26:43.023 --> 00:26:47.672
The steward records it before the
team leaves: who owns the repository

413
00:26:47.672 --> 00:26:52.520
and its protections, each drift
class, each severity and each review.

414
00:26:53.270 --> 00:26:56.370
The risk register starts
with five program risks.

415
00:26:56.370 --> 00:27:00.068
Hidden dependencies are missed
in discovery: the answer is a

416
00:27:00.068 --> 00:27:04.432
structured assessment and a pilot
designed to surface unknowns early.

417
00:27:04.432 --> 00:27:06.851
Competing sources of truth persist:

418
00:27:06.851 --> 00:27:10.720
one authoritative origin is
designated per object class.

419
00:27:10.720 --> 00:27:14.000
The organization resists
making authority explicit:

420
00:27:14.000 --> 00:27:18.520
the answer is early alignment, and
decision rights with one decider per row.

421
00:27:18.520 --> 00:27:22.075
Tooling leads the design:
the target model comes first,

422
00:27:22.075 --> 00:27:26.182
because tools bought first
automate the old ambiguity faster.

423
00:27:26.182 --> 00:27:29.838
And physics or data residency
limits centralization,

424
00:27:29.838 --> 00:27:34.795
so truth is centralized logically,
not necessarily in one physical store.

425
00:27:34.795 --> 00:27:37.645
Beneath them sit
twenty-five delivery risks.

426
00:27:37.645 --> 00:27:39.833
A few deserve executive attention.

427
00:27:39.833 --> 00:27:45.307
Critical certificate-services
misconfigurations can force emergency PKI work.

428
00:27:45.307 --> 00:27:50.345
Legacy applications depend on NTLM,
which Conditional Access can't evaluate,

429
00:27:50.345 --> 00:27:55.069
so each dependency becomes a finding
with an owner and a dated exception.

430
00:27:55.069 --> 00:27:58.596
Strong certificate mapping
is no longer a future risk:

431
00:27:58.596 --> 00:28:01.182
domain controllers already enforce it.

432
00:28:01.182 --> 00:28:04.860
And at the end, access records
can be lost at decommission,

433
00:28:04.860 --> 00:28:07.833
and governance can decay
once the team leaves.

434
00:28:07.833 --> 00:28:10.382
The register doesn't satisfy a control.

435
00:28:10.382 --> 00:28:13.920
It tells the agency when its
system security plan and its

436
00:28:13.920 --> 00:28:17.457
risk assessment have to be
updated, which is at every gate.

437
00:28:17.457 --> 00:28:19.920
The project manager keeps the register,

438
00:28:19.920 --> 00:28:24.101
the authorizing official accepts
residual risk, and security risk

439
00:28:24.101 --> 00:28:28.490
left open past a gate becomes a
plan of action and milestones item.

440
00:28:29.240 --> 00:28:31.940
So, the decisions, with their owners.

441
00:28:31.940 --> 00:28:36.651
The executive sponsor charters the
program as governance reconstruction,

442
00:28:36.651 --> 00:28:40.315
signs the charter, and puts names
in it as Phase 0's entry work.

443
00:28:40.315 --> 00:28:43.475
The sponsor and CIO fund
one sequenced program,

444
00:28:43.475 --> 00:28:47.284
with the AD DS retirement
phase and the people beyond the

445
00:28:47.284 --> 00:28:52.065
five-and-a-half-person core in the
budget, approved before design begins.

446
00:28:52.065 --> 00:28:55.829
And they build the steward and
steering committee from the zero

447
00:28:55.829 --> 00:28:59.665
trust lead and the ICAM governance
body the agency already has.

448
00:28:59.665 --> 00:29:03.192
The CISO's office confirms
the M-26-14 dates,

449
00:29:03.192 --> 00:29:09.365
starting with the Agency Logging Plan,
due November 18 by the program's count.

450
00:29:09.365 --> 00:29:14.668
The authorizing official decides whether
anything runs past end of support,

451
00:29:14.668 --> 00:29:18.065
beginning with Windows
Server 2016 on January 12.

452
00:29:18.065 --> 00:29:23.315
The steward sets the evidence and provenance
expectations before the first change,

453
00:29:23.315 --> 00:29:26.240
and clears G0 only when
every Critical finding

454
00:29:26.240 --> 00:29:29.390
is closed or accepted by
the authorizing official.

455
00:29:29.390 --> 00:29:32.125
The steward and sponsor record the pilot's

456
00:29:32.125 --> 00:29:35.328
thresholds before it opens,
and sign G2 on the data.

457
00:29:35.328 --> 00:29:40.081
The sponsor and CIO decide when each
Active Directory component retires,

458
00:29:40.081 --> 00:29:42.653
after the records officer is consulted.

459
00:29:42.653 --> 00:29:47.315
And the steward records the handover to
operations before the project team leaves.

460
00:29:47.315 --> 00:29:48.965
None of these is a purchase.

461
00:29:48.965 --> 00:29:54.074
The case for modernizing now is that the
estate's governance was never written down,

462
00:29:54.074 --> 00:29:58.368
the dates that bind it now belong to
others, and the only way to meet them

463
00:29:58.368 --> 00:30:03.403
without losing that governance is to recover
it deliberately, once, in one program.

464
00:30:03.403 --> 00:30:04.230
Thank you.
